1.3
Introduction to binomial trees
We presented in Section (1.1.1.2) European and American options and argued in Section (1.1.2) that their prices were
contingent on the dynamics of their underlying asset. Using elementary mathematics, the uncertainty of the underlying
stock price reduce to discrete binomial movements, leading to a binomial option pricing formula for both European
and American options. The binomial model was introduced into option pricing theory by Cox et al.
[1979], and
since then, it has been extensively covered in many textbooks on mathematical ﬁnance (see Shreve [2003], London
[2005]). Binomial models highlights the economic intuition behind option pricing theory in a simple way. In the
following sections, we are going to brieﬂy describe different binomial trees and introduce American pricing with
discrete dividends.
