1.6.3.1
Delta hedging
Through delta-hedging, we hope to remain immune to movements in the underlying asset price that may negatively
affect our position. Since the underlying asset has a Delta of one, we need only buy/sell the opposite number of units
of the underlying (relative to our position) in order to achieve Delta-neutrality of our portfolio. For example, consider
a trading strategy designed to isolate the skew in the volatility surface. When the implied risk-neutral density is more
negatively skewed than the "real" density, OTM put options are potentially overpriced relative to ATM call or put
options. Since skewness is known to be a mean-reverting process, a common approach is to "trade against the skew",
by selling OTM put options and buying ATM or NTM call or put options. Such a strategy is commonly known as
a Risk-Reversal. Consider a position short one put option at strike K1, and long one call option at strike K2 where
K1 < K2. Then, Delta-neutrality can be achieved by forming the following portfolio:
ΠRR = C(St, K2) −P(St, K1) −
