4.1.3.3
Accounting for other risks
Note, we have only considered local volatility models, but in principle we could also account for jumps in the underlying price, transaction costs, etc. Market practice is to take a simple model and add some reserve for additional risk
not accounted for by the model. This is part of model risk. If the trader has a view on the direction of the market
(mean-reverting), he can formulate an optimisation problem and solve a speciﬁc Hamilton Jacobi Bellman equation.
In case of a trending market, a shadow delta would sufﬁce.
